{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Six days shall work be done, etc.</b> Rashi explained: He placed the warning of the Sabbath before the command regarding the work of the Tabernacle, to say that it does not override the Sabbath. And the question is raised here: but in parashat Kedoshim (Leviticus 19:3) it is written ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ וְאֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״, and Rashi explained there: if your father says to you, desecrate the Sabbath, do not listen to him — and there He did not place the Sabbath first, and even so he explained that it overrides the honor of father and mother; whereas here Rashi made the matter depend on the fact that the Sabbath was placed before the work of the Tabernacle. <br>And it appears that this is not difficult at all, for there the inference is from the fact that it is written ״אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ in the plural, whereas it should have said ״תִּשְׁמוֹר״, since the verse begins in the singular — as it says ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ״, meaning every individual man. And as for the matter of fear of father and mother, granted that it began in the singular and then returned to say ״תִּירָאוּ״ in the plural — this was nevertheless necessary to teach that both a man and a woman are obligated in the fear of a parent, for one might have thought that since a woman is under another's authority she is exempt from fear of a parent, as Rashi explained. But regarding the Sabbath, why did it say ״תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ in the plural? For there is no reason to distinguish there between man and woman. Rather, it means to say: you and your son are both obligated in My honor — just as ״תִּירָאוּ״ implies two — a man and a woman, so too ״תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ implies two — you and your son. And if so, whenever Scripture places one commandment before its fellow, the earlier one is primary and overrides the later one. <br>And likewise in the verse ״אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ וּמִקְדָּשִׁי תִּירָאוּ״ (Leviticus 19:30), it also placed the Sabbath before the Sanctuary for this same reason. And it is different there, for the verse diminished [the implication] by saying ״תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ in the plural, and it did not reveal to us this [principle] by way of precedence — because it was necessary to place the fear of father and mother first in order to equate their fear with the fear of Heaven. <br>" "And the fact that in parashat Ki Tissa (Exodus 31:13) he placed the Tabernacle before the Sabbath and wrote ״אַךְ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ — to derive from the superfluity of the word ״אַךְ״ that the Tabernacle does not override the Sabbath — it should have placed the Sabbath first and not needed the superfluity of ״אַךְ״. Now, according to what we explained above in parashat Ki Tissa regarding the superfluity of the word ״אַךְ״, it was necessary to write the word ״אַךְ״. However, the words of Rashi will be resolved in another way, and along with this will also be resolved why Moses changed the order — for the Holy One, blessed be He, first commanded him concerning the Tabernacle, while Moses placed the Sabbath first. And this is because the Sabbath contains nothing but the honor of the Name, blessed be He, to indicate that He, blessed be He, created His world anew; whereas the Tabernacle indicates entirely the honor of Israel — that the Holy One, blessed be He, was reconciled with them and caused His Presence to dwell among them and forgave them the sin of the calf. And the Holy One, blessed be He, is exceedingly zealous for the honor of Israel; therefore He placed the Tabernacle first. And lest they err and say that this is why He placed it first — to say that the work of the Tabernacle overrides the Sabbath — therefore He added the word ״אַךְ״. But Moses reasoned that for the honor of the Name, blessed be He, it was fitting to place the Sabbath — which indicates His honor, blessed be He — first, and afterward the Tabernacle — which indicates the honor of Israel. And from this precedence it is understood on its own that the Sabbath overrides the work of the Tabernacle, for who is pushed aside before whom? You must say: the lesser before the greater. And above in parashat Ki Tissa you will find yet another reason why the Tabernacle is pushed aside before the Sabbath." "And the fact that the Holy One, blessed be He, in the Ten Commandments placed the commandment of the Sabbath before honoring father and mother — this was in order to set the commandments in parallel, five against five: for the command 'honor' corresponds to 'you shall not covet,' and it was necessary to seal the commandments with 'you shall not covet' to teach that all the commandments are included within 'you shall not covet,' and the end of a matter encompasses the whole. And this is clear and correct. And one should not object: from where does the conclusion arise that it would override, since the making of the Tabernacle is only a positive command, whereas the Sabbath contains both a positive command and a negative command and stoning? The answer to this is that even without this we find that the Sabbath is pushed aside with regard to the Tabernacle through the offerings that were brought on the day of the Sabbath; and were you to say that the Sabbath is pushed aside entirely with regard to the Tabernacle — it teaches us [otherwise]."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:32.609463686Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}